<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pithora painting</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pithora painting is a ritual wall-painting tradition of the Rathwa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhilala">Bhilala</a> communities of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Painted in bright colours on the inner walls of houses, often to fulfil a vow, the paintings show horses, riders, deities, animals and scenes of daily life, centred on the god Baba Pithora. They are made by men under the direction of a priest, accompanied by ritual and song.</p></div>
